<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · Love song · 1880 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Funiculì, Funiculà</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="nap">Funiculì, funiculà</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A cheerful Neapolitan song from 1880, written for the new funicular railway that carried people up the volcano Vesuvius.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Peppino Turco</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Luigi Denza</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Neapolitan</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Neapolitan</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Castellammare di Stabia, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1880</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Peppino Turco, published by Ricordi in 1880; music by Luigi Denza (d. 1922), in the public domain since 1993)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà</a>&quot; is a Neapolitan song composed in 1880 by Luigi Denza in his home town of Castellammare di Stabia, with words by the journalist Peppino Turco. It celebrated the opening that year of the first funicular railway on Mount Vesuvius, and it was Turco who prompted Denza to write it, perhaps as a joke. It was first sung at the Quisisana Hotel in Castellammare di Stabia and then presented by Turco and Denza at the Piedigrotta festival the same year. It became immensely popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and abroad: Casa Ricordi published the sheet music, which sold more than a million copies within a year.</p><p>The song was soon taken for a folk song. In 1884 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> publishers Spear &amp; Dehnhoff printed it with English words as &quot;Tra-la-la-lee&quot;, &quot;adapted and arranged by W. T. Harris&quot;, without naming Denza. Richard Strauss heard it on a tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> six years after it was written, thought it a traditional Neapolitan tune and used it in his tone poem Aus Italien; Denza sued him and won, and Strauss had to pay him a royalty. Nikolai Rimsky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korsakov-sakhalin">Korsakov</a> made the same mistake in his &quot;Neapolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; of 1907. Herman Bellstedt wrote a theme and variations on it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Napoli</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a> arranged it for ensemble in 1921.</p><p>In English-speaking countries it is often sung with words by Edward Oxenford, a lyricist and translator of librettos, whose version, often titled &quot;A Merry Life&quot;, has little to do with the original. Singers from Beniamino Gigli and Mario Lanza to Luciano Pavarotti and Andrea Bocelli have recorded it, and it has been sung in German and Japanese; in 1960 Robert B. and Richard M. Sherman wrote new English words to the tune as &quot;Dream Boy&quot;, which Annette Funicello released as a single. The melody has lived on in parodies, films, cartoons and video games, and since a children's parody of 1991 it has been linked with pizza and is often played in pizzerias. Denza died in 1922, and the song has been in the public domain since 1 January 1993.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A young man compares his sweetheart to a volcano and invites her to ride up with him to the top of Vesuvius on the new railway. The mood is high-spirited and teasing, a song of a day out and of courtship.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Neapolitan</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Neapolitan</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Aisséra, oje Nanniné, me ne sagliette,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Last night, oh Nannina, I went up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">tu saje addó, tu saje addó</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you know where, you know where,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Addó 'stu core 'ngrato cchiù dispietto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where this ungrateful heart can spite me</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">farme nun pò! (Farme nun pò)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>no more! (No more)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Addó lu fuoco coce, ma si fuje</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where the fire burns, but if you flee</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">te lassa sta! (Te lassa sta!)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it leaves you be! (It leaves you be!)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">E nun te corre appriesso, nun te struje</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And it does not run after you, it does not wear you away</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">sulo a guardà (sulo a guardà).</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>just by looking (just by looking).</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Jamme, jamme 'ncoppa, jamme jà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let's go, let's go up, let's go, come on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Jamme, jamme 'ncoppa, jamme jà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let's go, let's go up, let's go, come on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">funiculì, funiculà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>funiculì, funiculà!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">funiculì, funiculà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>funiculì, funiculà!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">'ncoppa, jamme jà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>up, let's go, come on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">funiculì, funiculà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>funiculì, funiculà!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Né, jamme da la terra a la muntagna!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Girl, let's go from the land to the mountain!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Nu passo nc'è! Nu passo nc'è!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is only a step! It is only a step!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Se vede Francia, Proceta e la Spagna...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One sees France, Procida and Spain...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Io veco a tte! Io veco a tte!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I see you! I see you!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Tirato co la fune, ditto 'nfatto,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Pulled by the rope, no sooner said than done,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">'ncielo se va, 'ncielo se va.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>up to the sky one goes, up to the sky one goes.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Se va comm' 'a lu viento a l'intrasatto,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One goes like the wind, all of a sudden,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">guè, saglie, sà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>hey, up it climbs, up [?]!</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 42 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Jamme, jamme 'ncoppa, jamme jà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let's go, let's go up, let's go, come on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Jamme, jamme 'ncoppa, jamme jà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let's go, let's go up, let's go, come on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">funiculì, funiculà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>funiculì, funiculà!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">funiculì, funiculà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>funiculì, funiculà!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">'ncoppa, jamme jà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>up, let's go, come on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">funiculì, funiculà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>funiculì, funiculà!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Se n'è sagliuta, oje né, se n'è sagliuta,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It has gone up, oh girl, it has gone up,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">la capa già! (La capa già!)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my head already! (My head already!)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">È gghiuta, po' è turnata, po' è venuta,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It went, then it turned back, then it came,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">sta sempe ccà! (Sta sempe ccà!)</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it is always here! (It is always here!)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">La capa vota, vota, attuorno, attuorno,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My head turns, turns, around, around,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">attuorno a tte! Attuorno a tte!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>around you! Around you!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Stu core canta sempe 'nt'o taluorno:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This heart always sings the same old refrain:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Spusamme, oje né! Spusamme, oje né!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let's get married, oh girl! Let's get married, oh girl!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Jamme, jamme 'ncoppa, jamme jà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let's go, let's go up, let's go, come on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">Jamme, jamme 'ncoppa, jamme jà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let's go, let's go up, let's go, come on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">funiculì, funiculà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>funiculì, funiculà!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">funiculì, funiculà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>funiculì, funiculà!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">'ncoppa, jamme jà,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>up, let's go, come on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="nap">funiculì, funiculà!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>funiculì, funiculà!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Peppino Turco (1880), the Neapolitan text as quoted on Italian Wikipedia. Text from <a href="https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Funicul%C3%AC_funicul%C3%A0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (10)</summary><ul><li>Funiculì, funiculà plays on funicolare, the funicular railway; the words are kept as they are.</li><li>Line 1.1: Nanninè, a pet form of Anna (Nannina); aisséra, 'yesterday evening'. Lines 3.1, 5.1 and 5.8: né, oje né, short for Nennè or Nanninè: 'girl', 'dear'.</li><li>Lines 1.3-1.4: literally 'where this ungrateful heart can do me no more spite'.</li><li>Line 1.5: the fire is the volcano's, on the mountain the funicular climbs.</li><li>Line 1.7: nun te struje, 'it does not consume (destroy) you'.</li><li>Refrain: jamme jà, 'let's go, come on'; 'ncoppa, 'up', 'to the top'.</li><li>Line 3.6: 'ncielo, 'to heaven' or 'into the sky'.</li><li>Line 3.8: guè is 'hey'; saglie, 'it climbs' or 'climb!'; sà is uncertain, perhaps a clipped repetition of saglie.</li><li>Lines 5.1-5.2: 'it has gone up, my head', i.e. the singer's head is in a whirl; capa, 'head'.</li><li>Line 5.7: 'nt'o taluorno, 'in the taluorno': a Neapolitan word for a tiresome, endless repetition or lament.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
