<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tra-la-la-lee</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1884</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English words to the melody, printed without naming Denza</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Aus Italien (Richard Strauss)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">uses the melody, taken for a folk song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Neapolitanskaya pesenka (Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1907</span><span class="sg-chain-h">uses the melody, taken for a folk song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Napoli (Herman Bellstedt)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">theme and variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Merry Life (Edward Oxenford)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English words, loosely based</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dream Boy</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new English words by Robert B. and Richard M. Sherman</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">My High Silk Hat</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1933</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody by Arthur Fields and Fred Hall</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Eat Until I Die</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1947</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tune adapted in the film Fun and Fancy Free</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pizza Song</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1991</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody by Franciscus Henri</span></li></ul></section>
